"In the opening verses of Surah Luqman, it is stated that the Qur'an is a guidance and mercy for the doers of good (muhsinun). In Surah al-Baqarah, it was described as guidance for the God-fearing; here, because the degree of ihsan (excellence) is higher, mercy has also been added. Our Prophet (peace be upon him) explained ihsan as \"worshiping Allah as if you see Him.\" Furthermore, the omission of the qualification of believing in the unseen in these verses demonstrates the elevated status of the muhsinun.\n\nIn verses 6–10, \"idle talk\" (lahw al-hadith) is condemned. This refers to things such as amusing tales, fables, and songs that divert people from the path of Allah. As the occasion of revelation for this verse, it is narrated that al-Nadr ibn al-Harith distracted people from the Qur'an with Persian tales and acquired a singing slave girl to entertain those who were inclined to become Muslims. Scholars differed over whether this verse also encompasses singing, yet the correct view is to take it in its general meaning. The expression \"without knowledge\" mentioned in the verse is linked, according to variant readings (qira'at), either to the verb \"purchases\" or considered a qualification of leading astray.\n\nIn verse 11, Allah's creation is pointed out, and people are asked to show what those besides Him have created, if indeed there are other creators. Worshiping entities that create nothing is a grave injustice. Here, a question may come to mind: Do humans not produce things through science and crafts? In response, it is emphasized that wisdom is a gift from Allah; therefore, the ultimate creator of what is made is Allah, and gratitude must be shown to Him. Polytheism (shirk) is the greatest injustice.\n\nIn verses 12–13, it is reported that wisdom (hikmah) was given to Luqman and that he was commanded to be grateful. Wisdom is reaching the correct goal by combining knowledge and action. Whether Luqman was a prophet is disputed; the majority hold the view that he was a sage. Wisdom was granted for gratitude; whoever is grateful is grateful for his own good, and whoever is ungrateful is ungrateful only to his own detriment. Then, Luqman's counsel to his son is recounted: \"O my dear son! Do not associate partners with Allah, for polytheism is indeed a grave injustice.\"\n\nVerses 14–15 command human beings to show goodness to their parents and to be grateful to them. However, if the parents compel one to associate partners with Allah, they must not be obeyed, though one should still maintain good companionship with them in worldly life. It is narrated that these verses were revealed concerning Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas and his mother. His mother opposed his becoming a Muslim and went on a hunger strike, whereas Sa'd risked everything for the sake of his religion. The verse defines the boundary of obedience to parents: obedience in matters that do not involve sin, and disobedience when it comes to polytheism.\n\nIn verses 16–19, Luqman's counsels to his son continue: Every deed, whether good or evil, even if it be the weight of a mustard seed, Allah will bring it forth. Establish the prayer, enjoin good, forbid evil, and bear patiently whatever befalls you. Do not be arrogant, do not walk swaggeringly upon the earth; maintain a moderate pace in your walking, and lower your voice. For indeed, the harshest of all sounds is the braying of donkeys. These counsels emphasize the transformation of wisdom into action and the importance of noble character.\n\nIn verses 20–30, Allah's blessings in the heavens and on the earth are recalled. Among people are those who dispute about Allah without knowledge and blindly follow their forefathers. Yet whoever turns to the truth has grasped the most trustworthy handhold. Even if the seas were ink and the trees were pens to write the words of Allah, they would not be exhausted. Creation and resurrection are as easy as the creation of a single soul. The merging of the night and day, and the sun and the moon running their courses for a specified term, demonstrate Allah's supreme power. Whatever is worshiped besides Him is falsehood.\n\nIn verses 31–33, the sailing of ships upon the sea is a blessing from Allah. When engulfed by waves, people plead with Allah in sincere devotion, but once saved, they show ingratitude. On the Day of Resurrection, neither a father can avail his child, nor can a child avail his father in any way. The life of this world must not deceive humanity, nor should Satan mislead them through Allah's forgiveness.\n\nIn verse 34, it is stated that knowledge of the Hour of Resurrection belongs solely to Allah, that He sends down the rain, that He knows whatever is in the wombs, and that no soul knows what it will earn tomorrow or in what land it will die. These five matters are the keys to the unseen. The Prophet (peace be upon him) recited this verse and stated that the keys to the unseen are five. However, Allah's granting a portion of this knowledge to whichever prophet or angel He wills does not contradict this exclusivity. This is because Allah's knowledge encompasses all things, whereas the knowledge of others is limited and based on evidence."